# FINMENTOR — line endings are part of the release contract, not a local preference.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.
#
# Several gates compare a TRACKED GENERATED ARTIFACT byte-for-byte against what its generator
# produces right now — that is the whole point of them, because an artifact nobody can reproduce
# is a number without a source. Every generator in this repository writes LF. Every blob in this
# repository is stored LF.
#
# On Windows, `core.autocrlf=true` (the default that ships with Git for Windows) rewrites those
# blobs to CRLF on checkout. It also rewrites them in `git archive`, which is how a "clean LF
# archive" used for release verification silently stopped being LF. The comparison then fails on
# the line endings alone, and the failure message — "the tracked artifact is stale relative to the
# generator" — says nothing about why. That is exactly how the canonical suite came to sit at
# 78/83 with an explanation attached, and how that explanation came to name the wrong cause: the
# four artifacts were never stale, and regenerating them would have changed nothing.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the WORKING TREE to LF on every platform, so a Windows checkout, a Linux CI
# checkout and `git archive` all produce the same bytes the generators write. No test is weakened,
# no floor is lowered and no comparison is loosened — the inputs simply stop being corrupted
# between the blob and the test.
#
# It does NOT touch what is stored: the blobs were already LF, so this changes no commit content.
#
# Binary formats are marked explicitly rather than left to `text=auto` detection. A PNG or a ZIP
# that autodetection got wrong would be corrupted by a line-ending pass, and "usually right" is
# not the standard for the release artifacts.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Sources, gates, generators and tracked artifacts — LF, always.
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.xml   text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
CNAME     text eol=lf
_headers  text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf

# PowerShell is run by Windows hosts. It tolerates LF, and pinning it with everything else keeps
# one rule rather than an exception nobody remembers.
*.ps1   text eol=lf

# Never transformed.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.zip  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
